#
# PEXT_SPEC.md section 7.2's build-time layer of the heterogeneity contract, spelled
# the way it asks for: one Kconfig symbol, one image, both behaviours.
#
# CONFIG_MB_PEXT=y makes fpga/pynq-z2/sw/pext.h emit the real custom-0 encodings; with
# it off, the identical sources compile against the header's bit-identical software
# model and run anywhere, including the LITTLE hart, spike and the build host.  The
# header reads CONFIG_MB_PEXT directly (Zephyr puts autoconf.h in front of every
# translation unit), so nothing else has to be wired up for this to take effect in the
# generated kernels.c.

mainmenu "ModelBlaster + MBP packed-integer extension"

config MB_PEXT
	bool "Build the ModelBlaster kernels against the MBP extension"
	default y
	help
	  Emit the real MBP.DOT8 / MBP.MAX8 / MBP.QMUL / MBP.CLIP8 encodings
	  (custom-0, opcode 0x0b) instead of pext.h's software model.

	  THE EXTENSION EXISTS ON HART 0 ONLY.  With this enabled, any thread
	  that reaches a curated pext kernel MUST be pinned to CPU 0 with
	  k_thread_cpu_pin(), or it takes an illegal-instruction trap.  That is
	  the intended behaviour and this sample tests for it deliberately;
	  see PEXT_SPEC.md section 7.

	  Turn this off to build the same image against the software model --
	  useful for bring-up on a bitstream that does not yet carry the
	  extension, and far slower than the plain scalar reference kernels, so
	  not a production configuration.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
